Certainly! I'll start by analyzing the `aggregateLogs` function for potential bottlenecks. Here's the current implementation:

```javascript
function aggregateLogs(logs) {
    return logs.reduce((agg, log) => {
        const date = new Date(log.timestamp).toISOString().split('T')[0];
        if (!agg[date]) {
            agg[date] = [];
        }
        agg[date].push(log);
        return agg;
    }, {});
}
```

To improve performance, I'll use a Map instead of an object to collect logs, which could offer better performance for large datasets due to faster key access:

```javascript
function aggregateLogs(logs) {
    const agg = new Map();
    logs.forEach(log => {
        const date = new Date(log.timestamp).toISOString().split('T')[0];
        if (!agg.has(date)) {
            agg.set(date, []);
        }
        agg.get(date).push(log);
    });
    return Object.fromEntries(agg);
}
```

This change should yield faster aggregation for larger logs. Next, I will run the tests to ensure everything is working correctly:

